 arrow shape,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:46:#   replaced by 8-connected size-3 arrowhead decoding.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:48:#   new symbolic cues, so the solver now decodes the arrowheads and template glyph\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:65:#   the paint cycle, side bars are the turn sequence, and the arrow/glyph direction\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:73:#   glyph stay in the top-left template region, while arrowheads stay inside the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:81:#   has exactly one indicator, the glyph direction matches every arrow marker, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:82:#   all placed arrows are non-degenerate against legacy neighbor-priority launch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:106:def _arrow_cells(tip_r: int, tip_c: int, direction: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:113:def _decode_arrow_component(coords: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int, str] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:120:            if set(_arrow_cells(int(tip_r), int(tip_c), direction)) == comp:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:157:        f\"arrow direction {marker_dir} must agree with template glyph {global_dir}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:162:def _find_arrow_markers(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:170:        decoded = _decode_arrow_component(coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:189:def _assert_arrow_markers_valid(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:196:    assert 1 <= len(markers) <= 2, f\"expected 1-2 arrow markers, got {len(markers)}\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:202:        assert tuple(sorted(_arrow_cells(tip_r, tip_c, direction))) == cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:310:    markers = _find_arrow_markers(g, int(pattern[0]), int(path_color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:311:    _assert_arrow_markers_valid(g, markers, int(path_color), str(global_dir))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:441:    Simulate recoloring particles on a path using arrow-shaped sources plus template direction symbols.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:446:    - Start markers are 3-cell arrowheads in the first cycle color, embedded inside the path band, and a separate 2x2 block provides the terminal collision color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:450:    - Decode each arrow marker and require its pointing direction to agree with the template glyph; each particle starts from the arrow tip in that shared direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:455:    - The template now carries two independent symbolic programs at once: block colors specify the repaint cycle, and instruction marks specify both the shared launch heading and the collision-turn schedule, while the arrowheads localize the actual start tips and confirm the heading geometrically.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:592:def _collect_arrow_candidates(mask: np.ndarray, target_row: int) -> list[dict[str, object]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:605:                    cells = _arrow_cells(int(r), int(c), direction)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:638:def _place_arrow_markers(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:641:    cand_lists = [_collect_arrow_candidates(mask, int(row)) for row in target_rows]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/22/code.py:782:    placed = _place_arrow_markers(g, mask, target_rows, int(cycle_colors[0]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/31/code.py:91:- The occupied non-background area is constrained to 10-20% of the grid, matching the sparse-narrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/6/code.py:26:#     The final box is two cells narrower and the surviving interior contains two bg holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/27/code.py:36:  otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable n×n placement before the first too-narrow gate\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/27/code.py:59:- Original assumption: a stuck case only comes from river self-narrowing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/898e7135/29/code.py:162:    assert w >= 2, \"grid too narrow for legend\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/898e7135/29/code.py:240:      colors themselves encode the slot's top-to-bottom order. Combining both signals narrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e376de54/30/code.py:56:#    Gap sizes remain in a narrow range so the board stays sparse and legible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:3:# Name: 显式分割线 + 连接器匹配 + 箭头标记反射\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:28:#   3. Reflection is read from arrow glyphs inside the matched target quadrant:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:29:#      right-arrow => LR flip, down-arrow => UD flip. Quadrant position no longer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:35:#     * connector + only down-arrow -> copy there, but apply UD instead of LR;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:36:#     * connector + both arrows -> copy there with both flips.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:59:#   arrows are instruction artifacts. They are stripped before motifs are stamped.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:66:#   regions are large enough for anchors, arrow glyphs, and at least one-cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:70:# - Active target anchors reserve a top band for arrows and at least one extra row\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:77:#   3. read the arrows in those same quadrants to decide the reflection bits.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:83:# - Colors are function-separated: divider=1, anchors=2, arrows=3, bg=4,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:97:#   quadrants with both a connector color repeated in TL and at least one arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:330:    Copy the TL motif into connector-matched regions, scaling by anchor size and mirroring by arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:335:    - Each active target shares a repeated connector color with a TL-side connector stub, and that same target region also contains one or two green arrow glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:339:    - Extract the TL motif while ignoring connector colors, then for each active target read its arrows: right-arrow means LR reflection, down-arrow means UD reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:342:    Key insight: The divider only tells you where the four regions are; connector colors decide which anchors participate at all, and arrow glyphs independently decide how each matched copy is reflected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:561:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:569:        _paint_arrow(grid, q_r0 + 1, q_c0 + 1, RIGHT_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:571:        _paint_arrow(grid, q_r0 + 1, q_c0 + 5, DOWN_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7c66cb00/2/code.py:59:    - B pieces are narrow rectangles placed far enough from the notch columns to avoid overlap inside bars\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/33/code.py:58:     colors, so the solver must detect the arrow-marked L component and the dock structurally.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/33/code.py:259:def _find_arrow_l_info(comp: dict) -> dict | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/33/code.py:371:        info = _find_arrow_l_info(comp)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/25/code.py:95:#   long-left area-6 branch, so the walk grammar does not collapse to a narrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:17:     Each strip shows one glyph sample on the left and one 5x5 arrow exemplar on the right:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:71:   - The 5x11 legend strips use a blank spacer column between glyph sample and arrow sample, keeping\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:82:   - Legend colors are purely stylistic: all legend shapes share one color, while arrow colors only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:83:     help distinguish arrow exemplars visually.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:89:     shape-vs-arrow samples; generator uses exactly that layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:134:# Right-side legend layout: one 5x5 glyph sample + spacer + one 5x5 arrow sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:272:    raise AssertionError(f\"unrecognized legend arrow patch:\\n{mask}\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:281:        arrow_patch = right[r0 : r0 + 5, LEGEND_ARROW_C0 : LEGEND_ARROW_C0 + 5]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:285:        glyph_to_dir[glyph_id] = _decode_dir_patch(arrow_patch, bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:307:      shows a glyph sample and a 5x5 arrow sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1