ft arrow with tip on the top ring at column c and free tail\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:39:#    arrow reading rule. Visual repetition is preserved by generating 2-3 arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:44:#      That fails here because each arrow has exactly one ring touch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:49:#      aggregates. That becomes irrelevant because arrows encode one L each.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:52:#    The generator uses output-first construction. For each arrow it first chooses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:67:#    still the cropped frame interior. Two-color arrows are common so start-order\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:71:#    Each arrow occupies a dedicated outer quadrant around one frame corner, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:73:#    arrows are separated from each other, the frame remains readable, and small\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:78:#    arrows the path is solid. For two-color arrows the solver uses only a visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:203:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:290:    Reconstruct interior corner paths from external bent arrows around a framed rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:294:    - Outside the frame, useful components are bent arrows: one pointed tip touches the frame's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:299:    - Map the arrow tip to the interior boundary startpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:301:    - Draw the corresponding Manhattan L-path inside the frame; monochrome arrows paint solidly,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a6f40cea/D3/0/code.py:302:      and two-color 